Inbound acknowledgement pipeline: EXPERIMENTAL PROTOTYPE, kept as a research artifact.
Never compiled by any target; it is the exact code the evaluation measured.
See docs/findings/nats-platform.md, "Inbound ack pipelining: evaluation".

Base: commit d22e245 plus this change's committed benchmark and probes
(internal/mqtt/inbound_ack_throughput_test.go, internal/mqtt/inbound_ack_order_test.go).
Apply on a scratch worktree of that tree:  git apply docs/findings/inbound-ack-pipeline-prototype.patch
Then: make bench-inbound, and the kill tests:
  GOMEMLIMIT=3000MiB go test -race -tags mqttport_fulltests -p 1 -run '^TestPipeKill' ./internal/mqtt/

Mutations (each applied alone to internal/mqtt/inbound_ack_pipe.go, each killed by
the named assertion; the unmutated prototype passes every kill test):
  P1 the issuer takes the first ready job, not the head
     -> TestPipeKillIssuerTakesTheHead: "the issuer acted on job N while job M was the head"
  P2 the emitter writes the first ready response, not the head's
     -> TestPipeKillEmitterWritesTheHead: "the emitter acted on job N while job M was the head"
  P3 a QoS 1 job marks its PUBACK ready at store submission
     -> TestPipeKillPubAckAwaitsTheStore: "PUBACK 1 enqueued before its $MQTT_msgs store returned"
  P4 a QoS 2 PUBLISH marks its PUBREC ready at hold submission
     -> TestPipeKillPubRecAwaitsTheHold: "PUBREC 1 enqueued before its hold CAS returned"
  P5 a release marks its PUBCOMP ready before the terminal CAS
     -> TestPipeKillPubCompAwaitsTheCommit: "PUBCOMP 1 enqueued before its terminal CAS returned"
  P6 every 1000th $MQTT_msgs store dropped, its PUBACK still sent
     -> TestMQTTInboundAckThroughput (standalone qos1 W=1): "stored 19980 records, acknowledged 20000"
  P7 the per-connection classification serialization removed
     -> TestPipeKillClassificationsTakeTurns: "release job(s) [4] were refused the record transaction ..."
The mutation script follows the patch as an appendix (after the last hunk; git apply
ignores it).

